<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n52883" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κάλυξ</span></b> 
		<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">[ᾰ]</span>, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ῠκος</span>, ἡ, also ὁ
		v.l. in
		<i><span class="en">
			Dsc.
			2.143
		</span></i>: (perh. cogn. with <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">καλύπτω</span>):— <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>covering</i>, used only of flowers and fruits:
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>1.</b>
			<i>seed-vessel, husk, shell</i> or <i>pod</i>, of the
			water-lily, <i><span class="en">
				Hdt.
				2.92
			</span></i>; of rice, <i><span class="en">
				Id.
				3.100
			</span></i>; of wheat, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">πρὶν ἂν ἐν τῇ κάλυκι
				γένηται</span> [<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἡ στάχυς</span>]
				<i><span class="en">
				Thphr.
				<u>HP</u>
				8.2.4
			</span></i>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				8.4.3
			</span></i>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κάλυκος ἐν λοχεύμασι</span>,
			i.e. when the fruit is setting, <i><span class="en">
				A.
				<u>Ag.</u>
				1392
			</span></i>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>OT</u>
				25
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Av.</u>
				1065
			</span></i> (lyr.). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>2.</b>
			<i>cup</i> or <i>calyx of a flower</i>, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀνεμωνῶν κάλυξι . . ἠριναῖς</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Cratin.
					98
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κισσοῖο καλύκεσσι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Theoc.
					3.23
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὅσα ἐν κάλυκι ἀνθεῖ</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Arist.
					<u>HA</u>
					554a12
				</span></i>
			; [<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">φύλλοις</span>] 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τοῖς τῶν ῥόδων ὅταν ἐν κάλυξιν ὦσι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Thphr.
					<u>HP</u>
					4.10.3
				</span></i>
			; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ῥόδου κ</span>. ibid.; so in
			Poets, <i>rosebud,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				h.Cer.
				427
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>AP</u>
				12.8
			</span></i> (<span class="entity">Strato</span>), etc.: metaph., 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">σταθερὰ . . κ. νεαρᾶς ἥβης</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Ar.
					<u>Fr.</u>
					467
				</span></i>
			. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b> in pl.,
				<i>women's ornaments</i>, perh. <i>ear-rings shaped like
				flower cups</i>, <i><span class="en">
				Il.
				18.401
			</span></i> (other expl. in Sch.), cf. <i><span class="en">
				<u>h.Ven.</u>
				87
			</span></i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>III.</b>
			
				=
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἄγχουσα</span>
			, <i><span class="en">
				Dsc.
				4.23
			</span></i>.</div>
